	        ,

			{
				"@type" : "Question",
				"name" : "<div>What was Eugene V. Debs\u2019s early life like? <\/div><em><\/em>",
				"acceptedAnswer" : {
					"@type" : "Answer",
					"text" : "At age 14, Eugene V. Debs began working in a railroad shop, later becoming a locomotive fireman. In 1875 he helped organize a local lodge of the Brotherhood of Locomotive Firemen, of which he was elected national secretary and treasurer in 1880. He was city clerk of <a href=\"https:\/\/www.britannica.com\/place\/Terre-Haute\">Terre Haute<\/a>, <a href=\"https:\/\/www.britannica.com\/place\/Indiana-state\">Indiana<\/a>, and a member of the Indiana legislature. "
				}
			}
	        ,

			{
				"@type" : "Question",
				"name" : "<div>What did Eugene V. Debs believe in?<\/div><em><\/em>",
				"acceptedAnswer" : {
					"@type" : "Answer",
					"text" : "Influenced by <a href=\"https:\/\/www.britannica.com\/biography\/Karl-Marx\">Karl Marx<\/a>, Eugene V. Debs was critical of traditional political and economic concepts, especially <a href=\"https:\/\/www.britannica.com\/money\/capitalism\">capitalism<\/a>. He saw the labour movement as a struggle between classes. After announcing his conversion to <a href=\"https:\/\/www.britannica.com\/money\/socialism\">socialism<\/a> in 1897, he led the establishment of the Socialist Party of America and was its candidate for <a href=\"https:\/\/www.britannica.com\/topic\/presidency-of-the-United-States-of-America\">U.S. president<\/a> five times between 1900 and 1920. "
